17592186065244 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065254 #edb/ref 1061 6061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065254 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb8aebaa-3368-514b-bf57-1953890130b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065254 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1064 "The **power rule for exponents** states that a power raised to another power is equal to the product of the two powers:\n\n$$\n\\left(a^m\\right)^n = a^{mn}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\\n\t\t\t\t5^6.\n\t}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power rule for exponents is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\left(5^2\\right)^3 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5^2\\cdot 5^2\\cdot 5^2}_{3\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n(5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) \\cdot (5 \\cdot 5) &= \\[5pt]\n\t\\underbrace{5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5\\cdot5}_{6\\text{ times}} &= \\[5pt]\n\t5^6\n}\n$$\n\nThe power rule for exponents is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1065 1480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a2e18f4-6996-52a0-95eb-77abb10d0e77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065338 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to remove the radical term in an expression like $\\sqrt{x^{3}}.$ To do this, we first convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}}= \\left(x^{3}\\right)^{1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we can apply the power rule and multiply the fractional exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{3})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {x}^{(3)\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^{3}} = x^{3/2},\n$$\n\nand in general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1065 1481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "40488864-6672-59b7-9ac6-79903856b484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065339 #edb/ref 1067 "The Power Rule With Fractional Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065342 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\na^2-b^2 = (a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nWe can use this formula to factor the difference of two squared monomials.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to factor the expression\n\n$$\nx^2 - 4\n$$\n\nThis expression is a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can factor the expression using the difference of squares formula with $a=x$ and $b=2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} - 2^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 2)(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065342 #edb/ref 1065 1599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065342 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3f50b677-50fc-5584-82de-7ad393d19d72" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065342 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065721 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1bc06e8b-9454-5e2b-82fd-21d690bf6ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065722 #edb/ref 1080 "7x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065722 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065722 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8de4a17a-b413-5d45-8140-74a4ed852096" #edb/ref 1759