2186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20d6dd44-7fc6-52a1-a076-27ddb6da9696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069730 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069730 #edb/ref 1061 2850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069730 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "818bd86a-fb35-5a26-8a36-9ee10c0aa1c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069730 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069731 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069731 #edb/ref 1061 2854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069731 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba820298-67c7-5655-858e-a20d665b5e15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069731 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069732 #edb/ref 1061 2857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069732 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "48e9774e-68ea-5a69-b5f7-f92416a589e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069732 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1061 4295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e24a880-f9da-5e1e-b0bf-5bb2128d72a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1061 4296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eea06921-8121-51a7-86ad-15824cc00550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069798 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1061 4299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7015e37-4cd2-5ffb-814e-264ca9a0b345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069799 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069858 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069858 #edb/ref 1061 4770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069858 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c18f2e00-ae66-5fd4-ab0a-9e795845b07b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069858 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1061 4905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65cce613-c74c-5057-9966-c834e52edbdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069869 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1064 "To simplify a rational expression, we cancel out the largest common factor from the numerator and denominator. To do this, we sometimes need to factor the numerator and denominator before canceling.\n\nFor example, suppose that we wish to simplify $\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}.$ To do this, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {4x - 12} {10x + 4}\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {4(x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (x - 3)} {2(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {\\cancel{2} \\cdot 2(x - 3)} {\\cancel{2}(5x + 2)}\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t&=\\dfrac {2(x - 3)} {5x + 2}\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1065 1370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "332f534f-811e-5c03-8393-560134dd4ea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069927 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since \n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x.$\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.$ Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.$\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,$ but for cube roots, we have $\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.$ No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1065 1605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ad1bc727-9054-5115-83f0-a82aa24dc35e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069942 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to solve equations that contain absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the *positive or negative* of the expression inside the absolute value must equal the right-hand side. That is,\n\n$$\nx+1 = 4 \\qquad\\qquad \\text{ or } \\qquad\\qquad -(x+1)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, let's solve these two equations:\n\n- First, we solve the equation $x+1 = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =4 \\\\ x+1-1 & =4-1 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation $-(x+1) = 4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+1) & =4 \\\\ -x-1 & =4 \\\\ -x-1+1 & =4+1 \\\\ -x & =5 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original absolute value equation are $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1065 8821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35d16431-4611-59fc-8888-c1db1a7b9902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070024 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we've seen that the solutions to the equation \n\n$$\n|x+1| = 4\n$$\n\nare $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}.$ \n\nWe can verify that these solutions are correct by substituting them back into the original equation and checking we get a true statement:\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{blue}3}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |3+1| & =4 \\\\ |4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\n- Substituting $x={\\color{red}-5}$ into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =4 \\\\ |-5+1| & =4 \\\\ |-4| & =4 \\\\ 4 & =4.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{red}-5}$ is indeed a solution to the absolute value equation.\n\nTherefore, $x={\\color{blue}3}$ and $x={\\color{red}{-5}}$ are both solutions to our absolute value equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1065 8822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "561ffe88-9340-5e0a-840a-6946f446a9f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070025 #edb/ref 1067 "Checking the Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186488272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Missing Angle in a Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073741 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fdb1e0fe-da89-5d80-b9a0-82d4daddc396" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
